        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How do I reset my Sole treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For a frozen console or an error, remove the safety key, switch off at the front power switch, unplug at the wall and wait at least two minutes before powering back on. On LED consoles, holding Stop for more than three seconds resets the console. To clear the LUBE message after lubricating, Sole's manuals say to power the treadmill on three times.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why won't my Sole treadmill turn on?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sole's diagnosis guide lists the causes in order: the safety key or tether is not in position, the circuit breaker on the front grill has tripped (push it in until it locks), the plug is out, or the house breaker has tripped. Only after those does it suggest a treadmill defect. Also check the power switch at the front, below the motor hood.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How often should I lubricate a Sole treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sole's 2026 manuals say every 90 hours of use or every 90 days, whichever comes first, using 2 to 3 ounces of Sole-approved 100% silicone lubricant. Check under the belt every other month and lubricate if it is dry, even if the LUBE light has not come on. Failing to lubricate regularly may void the warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What does E01 mean on a Sole treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              In Sole's 2026 F80 and F63 manuals, E01 is over-current protection: the treadmill is overloaded or something is stopping the motor turning. Power off and on, check nothing is jammed and that the deck is lubricated. If it returns or you smell burning, contact Sole service. Codes differ between model years, so check your own manual.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I fix a Sole treadmill belt that drifts to one side?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sole adjusts tracking on the left rear bolt only, with the belt running at 3 mph. If the belt is too close to the left, turn that bolt a quarter-turn clockwise; if too close to the right, counter-clockwise. Wait a few minutes after each turn, and keep hands away from the rear roller.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Where is the serial number on a Sole treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sole's support FAQ says it is a 16-digit number on a burgundy sticker just above the barcode, on the front of the lower base of the treadmill. You need it for warranty registration and for a service request.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is my Sole treadmill warranty transferable?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Sole's warranty and its support FAQ both say warranties apply to the original purchaser only and do not transfer, so a second-hand Sole has no warranty. Terms also vary by model and year: the 2026 F80 manual lists lifetime frame and motor, 3 years parts and 1 year labour.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
